1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,437
<font color="#FFFFFF">Time series pre-training promises to cut labeling
costs and boost many downstream tasks,</font>

2
00:00:06,517 --> 00:00:11,222
<font color="#FFFFFF">but the standard recipes borrowed from vision and
language fall short.</font>

3
00:00:11,302 --> 00:00:17,239
<font color="#FFFFFF">Randomly masking a series or computing series-wise
similarity tends to distort or ignore</font>

4
00:00:17,319 --> 00:00:21,273
<font color="#FFFFFF">the temporal correlations that make time series
meaningful.</font>

5
00:00:21,353 --> 00:00:23,392
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces TimeSiam,</font>

6
00:00:23,472 --> 00:00:28,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">a simple but effective self-supervised framework
built on Siamese networks.</font>

7
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:30,708
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of masking in isolation,</font>

8
00:00:30,788 --> 00:00:36,792
<font color="#FFFFFF">TimeSiam samples a past and a current subseries
from the same sequence and trains Siamese</font>

9
00:00:36,872 --> 00:00:41,442
<font color="#FFFFFF">encoders to reconstruct the masked current series
from the past one,</font>

10
00:00:41,522 --> 00:00:45,475
<font color="#FFFFFF">explicitly modeling how observations correlate
across time.</font>

11
00:00:45,555 --> 00:00:52,449
<font color="#FFFFFF">Learnable lineage embeddings let a single model
capture correlations at many different time distances.</font>

12
00:00:52,529 --> 00:00:58,192
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across thirteen benchmarks and two mainstream
tasks, forecasting and classification,</font>

13
00:00:58,272 --> 00:01:03,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">TimeSiam sets a new state of the art in both
in-domain and cross-domain settings.</font>

14
00:01:04,110 --> 00:01:08,231
<font color="#FFFFFF">Self-supervised pre-training has transformed vision
and language,</font>

15
00:01:08,311 --> 00:01:13,595
<font color="#FFFFFF">and researchers naturally reached for the same
tools when working with time series.</font>

16
00:01:13,675 --> 00:01:15,081
<font color="#FFFFFF">But the fit is awkward.</font>

17
00:01:15,161 --> 00:01:18,377
<font color="#FFFFFF">When you randomly mask points across a time series,</font>

18
00:01:18,457 --> 00:01:23,095
<font color="#FFFFFF">you can shatter the smooth temporal structure that
the signal depends on,</font>

19
00:01:23,175 --> 00:01:27,231
<font color="#FFFFFF">sometimes making reconstruction so hard the model
learns little.</font>

20
00:01:27,311 --> 00:01:33,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">Contrastive learning has the opposite problem:
comparing whole series for similarity tends</font>

21
00:01:33,128 --> 00:01:36,409
<font color="#FFFFFF">to ignore the fine-grained correlations inside them,</font>

22
00:01:36,489 --> 00:01:42,225
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it hinges on augmentations that are notoriously
difficult to design for temporal data.</font>

23
00:01:42,305 --> 00:01:47,783
<font color="#FFFFFF">The gap this paper addresses is clear: existing
recipes fail to emphasize the temporal</font>

24
00:01:47,863 --> 00:01:50,950
<font color="#FFFFFF">correlations that make time series what they are.</font>

25
00:01:51,330 --> 00:01:55,879
<font color="#FFFFFF">The timing could not be better for a method that
gets this right.</font>

26
00:01:55,959 --> 00:02:00,508
<font color="#FFFFFF">Every day the world's sensors, wearables,</font>

27
00:02:00,588 --> 00:02:05,137
<font color="#FFFFFF">and industrial systems pour out staggering volumes
of unlabeled time series through the Internet of Things.</font>

28
00:02:05,217 --> 00:02:09,766
<font color="#FFFFFF">That data is a gold mine, but only if we can learn
from it without hand labeling.</font>

29
00:02:09,846 --> 00:02:14,394
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key insight motivating TimeSiam is that time
series carry a special kind of information</font>

30
00:02:14,474 --> 00:02:19,023
<font color="#FFFFFF">prior methods throw away: the correlation between
what happened in the past and what is</font>

31
00:02:19,103 --> 00:02:23,652
<font color="#FFFFFF">happening now.</font>

32
00:02:23,732 --> 00:02:28,281
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of treating each window in isolation,</font>

33
00:02:28,361 --> 00:02:32,910
<font color="#FFFFFF">why not build a pre-training task that explicitly
asks the model to relate distant moments in time to each other.</font>

34
00:02:33,290 --> 00:02:37,194
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper makes three main contributions.</font>

35
00:02:37,274 --> 00:02:41,178
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it proposes TimeSiam,</font>

36
00:02:41,258 --> 00:02:45,162
<font color="#FFFFFF">a simple but effective pre-training framework that
uses Siamese networks to capture correlations</font>

37
00:02:45,243 --> 00:02:49,147
<font color="#FFFFFF">among temporally distanced subseries.</font>

38
00:02:49,227 --> 00:02:53,131
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it introduces learnable lineage embeddings,</font>

39
00:02:53,211 --> 00:02:57,115
<font color="#FFFFFF">a lightweight mechanism that lets one model
represent many different past-to-current time distances.</font>

40
00:02:57,195 --> 00:03:01,099
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

41
00:03:01,179 --> 00:03:05,083
<font color="#FFFFFF">through extensive experiments it shows TimeSiam
achieves consistent state-of-the-art results</font>

42
00:03:05,163 --> 00:03:09,068
<font color="#FFFFFF">when fine-tuned on both forecasting and classification,</font>

43
00:03:09,148 --> 00:03:13,052
<font color="#FFFFFF">and across both in-domain and cross-domain settings.</font>

44
00:03:13,132 --> 00:03:17,036
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, the framework is backbone-agnostic,</font>

45
00:03:17,116 --> 00:03:21,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">dropping cleanly onto modern encoders like
iTransformer, PatchTST, and TCN.</font>

46
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:23,164
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is how TimeSiam works.</font>

47
00:03:23,244 --> 00:03:29,992
<font color="#FFFFFF">From a single time series it randomly samples two
windows: a past subseries and a current subseries,</font>

48
00:03:30,072 --> 00:03:32,313
<font color="#FFFFFF">together called Siamese subseries.</font>

49
00:03:32,393 --> 00:03:37,981
<font color="#FFFFFF">The current window is lightly corrupted with a
simple augmentation such as masking.</font>

50
00:03:38,061 --> 00:03:42,897
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two shared-weight Siamese encoders process the past
and current windows,</font>

51
00:03:42,977 --> 00:03:50,544
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a decoder with cross-attention reconstructs the
masked current series using the past observation as context.</font>

52
00:03:50,624 --> 00:03:55,187
<font color="#FFFFFF">That single design choice, reconstructing the
current from the past,</font>

53
00:03:55,267 --> 00:04:00,035
<font color="#FFFFFF">forces the model to internalize how distant moments
in a series relate.</font>

54
00:04:00,115 --> 00:04:05,634
<font color="#FFFFFF">To handle the fact that past and current windows
can be near or far apart in time,</font>

55
00:04:05,714 --> 00:04:10,892
<font color="#FFFFFF">TimeSiam adds learnable lineage embeddings indexed
by that relative distance,</font>

56
00:04:10,972 --> 00:04:14,443
<font color="#FFFFFF">so one model fluently represents many temporal gaps.</font>

57
00:04:14,523 --> 00:04:22,090
<font color="#FFFFFF">The training objective is simply the squared
reconstruction error between the true and predicted current series.</font>

58
00:04:22,470 --> 00:04:26,193
<font color="#FFFFFF">The evaluation is deliberately broad,</font>

59
00:04:26,273 --> 00:04:29,996
<font color="#FFFFFF">spanning thirteen benchmarks and two mainstream tasks.</font>

60
00:04:30,076 --> 00:04:33,799
<font color="#FFFFFF">For forecasting there are the four ETT subsets,
plus Weather, Electricity, Traffic,</font>

61
00:04:33,879 --> 00:04:37,602
<font color="#FFFFFF">and Exchange.</font>

62
00:04:37,682 --> 00:04:41,405
<font color="#FFFFFF">For classification there are two EEG datasets, AD
and TDBrain, and an ECG dataset,</font>

63
00:04:41,485 --> 00:04:45,208
<font color="#FFFFFF">PTB.</font>

64
00:04:45,288 --> 00:04:49,012
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top of these eleven established benchmarks,</font>

65
00:04:49,092 --> 00:04:52,815
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors construct two new large-scale,</font>

66
00:04:52,895 --> 00:04:56,618
<font color="#FFFFFF">multi-domain datasets called TSLD-500M and TSLD-1G.</font>

67
00:04:56,698 --> 00:05:00,421
<font color="#FFFFFF">The larger one packs nearly fourteen million
examples drawn from diverse,</font>

68
00:05:00,501 --> 00:05:04,224
<font color="#FFFFFF">non-overlapping domains,</font>

69
00:05:04,304 --> 00:05:08,027
<font color="#FFFFFF">which lets the paper stress-test cross-domain
transfer where pre-training and fine-tuning</font>

70
00:05:08,107 --> 00:05:11,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">data come from entirely different sources.</font>

71
00:05:12,210 --> 00:05:14,806
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results are consistent and strong.</font>

72
00:05:14,886 --> 00:05:16,567
<font color="#FFFFFF">On in-domain forecasting,</font>

73
00:05:16,647 --> 00:05:23,117
<font color="#FFFFFF">TimeSiam cuts average mean squared error by five
point seven percent with a PatchTST backbone</font>

74
00:05:23,197 --> 00:05:26,287
<font color="#FFFFFF">and two point five percent with iTransformer,</font>

75
00:05:26,367 --> 00:05:31,146
<font color="#FFFFFF">and remember these backbones already forecast very
well from scratch.</font>

76
00:05:31,226 --> 00:05:33,118
<font color="#FFFFFF">On in-domain classification,</font>

77
00:05:33,198 --> 00:05:38,894
<font color="#FFFFFF">it lifts average accuracy by eleven point five
percent over random initialization.</font>

78
00:05:38,974 --> 00:05:44,528
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across all these settings TimeSiam beats eight
strong self-supervised baselines.</font>

79
00:05:44,608 --> 00:05:50,797
<font color="#FFFFFF">Perhaps the most striking finding is in
cross-domain transfer: pre-training on the large,</font>

80
00:05:50,877 --> 00:05:57,417
<font color="#FFFFFF">diverse TSLD-1G dataset and fine-tuning elsewhere
sometimes beats even in-domain pre-training,</font>

81
00:05:57,497 --> 00:06:02,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">which confirms that scale and diversity of
pre-training data really pay off.</font>

82
00:06:03,150 --> 00:06:08,329
<font color="#FFFFFF">Careful ablations on the Traffic benchmark tell us
which design choices matter.</font>

83
00:06:08,409 --> 00:06:14,321
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reconstructing the current window from a past one
clearly beats plain self-reconstruction,</font>

84
00:06:14,401 --> 00:06:16,518
<font color="#FFFFFF">validating the core Siamese idea.</font>

85
00:06:16,598 --> 00:06:22,576
<font color="#FFFFFF">The masking ratio has a sweet spot around
twenty-five percent: masking only fifteen percent</font>

86
00:06:22,656 --> 00:06:25,838
<font color="#FFFFFF">makes the task too easy to teach anything useful,</font>

87
00:06:25,918 --> 00:06:29,366
<font color="#FFFFFF">while masking seventy-five percent makes it too hard.</font>

88
00:06:29,446 --> 00:06:34,092
<font color="#FFFFFF">Lineage embeddings deliver consistent gains over
random initialization,</font>

89
00:06:34,172 --> 00:06:40,017
<font color="#FFFFFF">and adding more of them keeps improving results on
Electricity and Traffic up to a point,</font>

90
00:06:40,097 --> 00:06:45,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">confirming that explicitly modeling multiple
temporal distances is worthwhile.</font>

91
00:06:45,590 --> 00:06:51,446
<font color="#FFFFFF">To put the impact in numbers: TimeSiam reduces
average forecasting error by five point</font>

92
00:06:51,526 --> 00:06:56,209
<font color="#FFFFFF">seven percent on PatchTST and two point five
percent on iTransformer,</font>

93
00:06:56,289 --> 00:07:02,352
<font color="#FFFFFF">and raises classification accuracy by eleven point
five percent in the in-domain setting,</font>

94
00:07:02,432 --> 00:07:04,974
<font color="#FFFFFF">all relative to training from scratch.</font>

95
00:07:05,054 --> 00:07:10,979
<font color="#FFFFFF">It does this across thirteen benchmarks covering
forecasting and classification in both</font>

96
00:07:11,059 --> 00:07:13,464
<font color="#FFFFFF">in-domain and cross-domain settings,</font>

97
00:07:13,544 --> 00:07:18,158
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it outperforms eight state-of-the-art
self-supervised baselines.</font>

98
00:07:18,238 --> 00:07:21,057
<font color="#FFFFFF">Backing the cross-domain story is TSLD-1G,</font>

99
00:07:21,137 --> 00:07:27,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">a newly built pre-training dataset with nearly
fourteen million examples spanning multiple domains.</font>

100
00:07:28,270 --> 00:07:30,584
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting takeaway is a reframing.</font>

101
00:07:30,664 --> 00:07:36,901
<font color="#FFFFFF">By posing time series pre-training as
past-to-current reconstruction between Siamese subseries,</font>

102
00:07:36,981 --> 00:07:43,218
<font color="#FFFFFF">and equipping the model with learnable lineage
embeddings to span different temporal distances,</font>

103
00:07:43,298 --> 00:07:49,802
<font color="#FFFFFF">TimeSiam captures exactly the correlations that
masking and contrastive methods leave on the table.</font>

104
00:07:49,882 --> 00:07:54,789
<font color="#FFFFFF">The payoff is a simple, general framework that
drops onto modern backbones,</font>

105
00:07:54,869 --> 00:07:57,515
<font color="#FFFFFF">scales with larger and more diverse data,</font>

106
00:07:57,595 --> 00:08:02,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">and sets a new state of the art for transfer across
tasks and domains.</font>
